This project explores the application of Kalman Filters—specifically the Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) and Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF)—for the autonomous navigation and precise localization of mobile robots. Implemented in Python, the system estimates the robot's position, orientation, and velocity by processing noisy sensor data from a velocity sensor, gyroscope, and GNSS (GPS). The project evaluates different observation models (such as dead reckoning) and visualizes the estimated trajectories against true paths. The results demonstrate that while the EKF is computationally efficient, the UKF provides superior accuracy in highly non-linear scenarios with significant noise
